# Untitled

## General Synthesis and Solution Processing of Metal–Organic Framework Nanofibers
By virtue of their extraordinarily high surface areas, ordered pore structures, various compositions, and rich functionality, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are of great interest in diverse fields such as gas separation, sensing, catalysis, energy, environment science, and biomedicine. However, the difficulty in processing MOF crystals and controlling the MOF superstructure is emerging as a critical issue in their application. Herein, it is reported that a robust template, i.e., nanofibrillated cellulose (NFC), can be used for the synthesis of MOF materials with 1D nanofiber morphology. NFC@MOF core–shell nanofibers with a uniform network structure and high aspect ratios can be prepared by use of this template. The small crystal size, flexibility, and good dispersity of the NFC@MOF nanofibers make it convenient for the macroscale assembly and solution processing of MOF materials. A proof-of-concept study is demonstrated wherein freestanding MOF nanofiber membranes represent good performance in applications of water treatment and heterogeneous catalysis reaction. This general synthesis and solution-processing strategy may herald a new era in promoting the industrial application of MOFs.
## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are high-surface-area coordination polymers with regularly porous structures and adjustable functionalities,[1–4] research on MOFs has rapidly progressed from studying aspects of their fundamental chemistry to pursuing applications in the multidisciplinary areas of nanotechnology and materials science.[5–8] However, the fragile nature of MOFs, where MOFs easily break down into powders, holds back their effective usage as versatile materials in various applications such as sensing, catalysis, energy, and in gas storage or separation.[5,9–13] Macroscopic forms of MOFs, such as membranes, are being investigated to transform
laboratory-synthesized MOF powders to industrially viable products for separations, chemical sensors, and catalysts.[14,15] Two main fabrication strategies, have been developed for MOF membranes. The first method involves the preparation of continuously grown films, composed of an uninterrupted, pure-MOF thin layer on a supporting substrate with a complicated process.[16,17] The second method involves composite systems, wherein the MOFs are combined with a polymer, usually as mixed-matrix membranes,[18–20] these MOFs in the form of either a microcrystalline powder or small crystals in the sub-millimeter regime are not inherently suitable for membrane processes. Thus, processable MOFs have been long sought to advance their applications in various technologies from separations to catalysis. Meanwhile, the preparation of size- and shape-controlled MOF nanocrystals and the assembly of MOFs in macro
scale materials, for example, functional MOF thin films or membranes, are main challenges at present.[21–24] On this account, searching for new strategies for controlling the sizes and dimensions of the MOF crystals suitable for solution processing has attracted a lot of concerns.[25–27] Although 1D MOFs have advantages in assembling macroscopic membrane materials via convenient solution processing course, there are few methods to synthesize 1D MOFs superstructures for lack of highly uniform 1D nanomaterial templates. The crystalline of MOFs usually leads to a mechanical fragigibility such that flexible freestanding MOF membranes are difficultly obtained. At present, MOF nanofibers are in most cases obtained via electrospinning with polymer composite at the micrometer-scale.[28–30] Besides, MOF nanocrystals are always incorporated in polymer membranes to design mixed matrix membranes for improved selective separation performance.[18,19,31,32] While 1D MOFs have been fabricated by the assembly of MOFs onto several 1D template nanomaterials recently,[33,39] it remains drawbacks such as high-cost templates, as-synthesized nanoparticles with low aspect ratios, large crystal size, and a low mass fraction of MOFs (Figure S1, Supporting Information).
As the most abundant and sustainable biopolymer on the earth, cellulose-based 1D nanomaterial would be an ideal template for the synthesis of MOF nanofibers attribute to the presence of functional groups such as carboxyl and hydroxyl species. The functional groups enable the interactions with
the metal ions, which is extremely important for the synthesis of MOF nanofibers. Accordingly, we intend to carry out template synthesis methodology studies of high-aspect-ratio MOF nanofibers by using cellulose, and then assemble MOF nanofibers into macroscopic materials through solution processing. The maintained 1D morphology and network structure of synthetic MOF nanomaterials facilitate processing MOFs into freestanding membranes or coating MOFs on different substrate materials for a range of applications. These 1D MOF nanofibers can replace the traditional MOF nanoparticles, providing new means and possibilities for the assembly and industrial application of MOF macroscopic materials.
## 2. Results and Discussion
Figure 1 schematically illustrates a general method for the scalable synthesis of MOF nanofibers including ZIF-8, UiO-66, and MIL-53(Al) by employing nanofibrillated celluloses (NFCs), a sustainable bio-based nanomaterials, as templates. The prepared ZIF-8, UiO-66, and MIL-53(Al) nanofibers possess a high aspect ratio of more than 100 and thus can be easily assembled into diverse macroscopic materials including MOF nanofiber-coated tubular membranes, plastic meshes and freestanding MOF nanofiber membranes by conventional solution processing techniques, such as dip-coating, air-spraying, and suction filtration. We also demonstrate the application potentials of the freestanding MOF nanofiber membranes for continuous flow catalysis and rapid removal of organic micropollutants from water.
We first prepared the NFC templates from tunicate by TEMPO-mediate oxidation of the raw materials followed with mechanical disintegration. The primary hydroxyl groups facing the outer side of each cellulose fibril (i.e., about one of every
We first prepared the NFC templates from tunicate by TEMPO-mediate oxidation of the raw materials followed with mechanical disintegration. The primary hydroxyl groups facing the outer side of each cellulose fibril (i.e., about one of every two glucose units of the cellulose chains on the cellulose fibril surface) were oxidized to the carboxylate ones by the TEMPO-mediated oxidation.[40,41] When the carboxylate groups formed from the C6 primary hydroxyl groups of bleached tunicin, the oxidized cellulose/water slurries were converted to transparent and highly viscous dispersions by mechanical disintegration treatment using a household blender. The atomic force microscopy (AFM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images demonstrate that NFCs obtained from tunicate (TNFC) have higher aspect ratio than wood cellulose nanofibers (WNFC, a few micrometers length and \(3{-}5\,\mathrm{~nm}\) width) and more uniform diameter than bacterial cellulose nanofibers (BNFC, more than a dozen micrometers length and \(20{-}40\ \mathrm{nm}\) width), which states that tunicate NFCs are optimal templates to synthesis high-quality MOF nanofibers (Figure 2a; Figures S2 and S3, Supporting Information). AFM observation of the NFCs indicated that the tunicate cellulose was totally converted to individual nanofibers with a length of more than dozens of micrometers and a uniform width of around \(10\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure S4, Supporting Information). Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) spectra of the NFC films supported that the \(-\mathrm{COOH}\) groups were leading into the cellulose molecular chain. The absorbance peak around \(1722\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in Figure S5 of the Supporting Information is thus primarily due to stretching vibration of \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{O}\) in carbonyl groups. The rich hydroxyl and carboxylate groups of NFCs can supply numerous sites for MOF heterogeneous nucleation.[42,43] The FTIR spectra of NFC templates after the reaction with different metal ions further confirmed the coordination between \(-\mathrm{COOH}\) groups and metal ions (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Besides, NFCs are believed to be hydrophilic due to the polar hydroxyl groups but hydrophobic faces also exist due to the exposure of hydrophobic \(-\mathrm{CH}\) moieties. Although the hydrophobic surfaces are less representative, cellulose still possesses the
property to capture nonpolar solvents such as chloroform or cyclohexane and interact with the hydrophobic aromatic moieties. A cellulose crystal in an elementary fibril owns both hydrophobic and hydrophilic faces, among them, the (200) face refers to the hydrophobic face while the (0–10), (110), (1–10), and (010) refer to hydrophilic faces.[44] On account of amphiphilic properties, NFCs can be individually dispersed in polar aprotic organic solvents such as N,N-dimethylacetamide (DMAc), N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), 1,3-dimethyl-2-imidazolidinone (DMI), and 1-methyl-2-pyrrolidinone (NMP). Furthermore, the carboxyl groups of the NFCs are sufficiently dissociated in the organic solvents, allowing individually dispersed NFCs to form in organic solvents by electrostatic repulsion.[45] Such unique surface chemistry would make NFCs be ideal templates to synthesize diverse 1D flexible MOF nanofibers with high aspect ratios for solution processing.
By the NFC-templating synthesis method, we first prepared ZIF-8 nanofibers in methanol, UiO-66 and MIL-53(Al) in DMF. The latter two MOFs structures could be used to synthesize UiO-66-NH2 and MIL-53(Al)-NH2 nanofibers by replacing the terephthalic acid ligand with 2-amino-terephthalic acid. The detailed synthetic recipes and steps are described in the Experimental Section. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images indicate that the prepared MOF nanofibers displayed a typical 1D nanostructure with a uniform diameter of about 50 nm (Figure 2b–d). The size and morphology of UiO-66-NH2, and MIL-53(Al)-NH2 nanofibers retained similar after ligand changing (Figure 2e,f). All of the MOF nanofibers inherited the length of NFCs template of more than a dozen micrometer, corresponding to a high aspect ratio of more than 100. Besides, we can clearly observe the core–shell nanofiber structure
composed of NFCs core with low contrast in the TEM images (Figures S7–S9, Supporting Information).
X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns show that the diffraction peaks of ZIF-8, UiO-66, and MIL-53(Al) nanofibers match well with the simulated patterns from CIF files of the corresponding structures. The broadening of the diffraction peaks relative to the simulated ones suggests the small crystalline size of the prepared MOFs (Figure 2g–i). The nitrogen adsorption-desorption measurements at 77 K were then performed to analyze the porous characteristics of the MOF nanofibers (Figure 2j). The isotherm of MIL-53(Al)-NH2 nanofiber that has only a slight increase at low pressure should belong to type II. The lower Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area and micropore volume coincide its XRD pattern reflecting a low crystallinity. In addition, other MOF nanofibers exhibit mixture isotherms of type I and II with the rapid increases of nitrogen adsorption at low pressures. The adsorption curves are concave upward at \({\approx}0.9P/P_{0}\) indicating the existence of macropores, which can be attributed to the nanofiber network structures. There are none of obvious inconspicuous hysteresis loops occur at higher \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) pressures, which reflect the absence of mesopores in MOF nanofibers. The pore size distribution (PSD) curves based on the NLDFT method further confirm the microporous feature of the MOF nanofibers (Figure 2k). The apparent BET surface areas \((S_{\mathrm{BET}})\) are 715.8, 858.6, 982.2, 1016.1, and \(2171\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for ZIF-8, UiO-66, UiO-66-NH2, MIL-53(Al), and MIL-53(Al)-NH2 nanofibers, respectively, with the pore volumes in the range of \(0.694{-}0.997~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (Table S1, Supporting Information). The BET surface areas of the original ZIF-8 nanoparticles and NFCs are 978.8 and \(51.3\;\mathrm{~m}^{2}\;\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\) (Figure S10, Supporting Information). The BET surface area of ZIF-8 nanofibers \((715.8\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\)
just has a slight decrease because the NFC templates do not make contributions. The high surface area of MOF nanofibers is attributed to the high loading mass of MOFs on the NFCs. We then performed the thermogravimetry measurements under the air flow to analyze the loading capacities of MOFs on NFCs (Figure S11, Supporting Information). At \(700\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the final residues are considered to be the corresponding metal oxides. The loading mass of ZIF-8, UiO-66, UiO-66-NH\(_2\), MIL-53(Al), and MIL-53(Al)-NH\(_2\) are 90.7, 85.5, 89.8, 75.9, and \(82.6~\mathrm{wt\%}\), respectively.
The versatility of the NFC-templating synthesis allows the large-scale synthesis of MOF nanofibers possible by applying continuous flow technology, which has been recognized as an efficient synthetic method for large-scale synthesis of nanostructured materials for practical applications.[46–48] In addition, the general advantages of the continuous flow technology, like efficient mass transfer and feedback control of streams, make it easier to precisely control the mass feeding, reaction temperature and reaction time. By taking ZIF-8 as an example, we show the large-scale synthesis of MOF nanofibers at ambient condition and the regulation of their size by the continuous-flow technology (Figure 3a). Briefly, \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and 2-methylimidazole (MeIM) were separately dissolved in methanol solution (MeOH) to obtain the precursor solutions. To the as-prepared metal salt solution, calculated amounts of NFC template were added. A coiled silicone tubular reactor (inner diameter: \(3\,\textrm{m m}\)) was connected via a fluidic programmable
peristaltic pump (KSP-F01A, Kamoor Fluid Tech (Shanghai) Co., Ltd.) to transport the reaction mixtures at designated flow rates corresponding to different reaction time. The ZIF-8 nanofibers were collected by centrifugation and washed for several times and dispersed into ethanol solution for subsequent use (Figure 3b). Streams were supplied into two silicone tubes by peristaltic pumps. Once the streams converged in the reaction region, mixed streams were smoothly passed through and the reaction time was adjusted by the flow rate and the length of a silicone tube reactor. SEM observation revealed that highly uniform ZIF-8 nanofibers were obtained by the continuous flow method. At a fixed flow rate of \(5~\mathrm{mL~min}^{-1}\), the ZIF-8 nanofibers collected at different time have a similar diameter of around \(50~\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 3c), indicating the high reliability of the method. Importantly, the diameter of the ZIF-8 nanofibers could be easily controlled in a wide range of \(20{-}100\ \mathrm{nm}\) by slowing down the flow rates from 10 to \(1\,\mathrm{mL\,min}^{-1}\) (Figure 3d). Clearly, the diameter of ZIF-8 nanofibers is positively correlated to the residence time in the tube reactor.
The flexible structure feature and high aspect ratio of the prepared MOF nanofibers enable them with outstanding solution processability for making macroscopic MOF materials. The ZIF-8 nanofibers prepared by the continuous flow method can be well dispersed in solvent to form a homogeneous suspension (Figure 3b), which then could be processed to diverse macroscopic materials by different solution processing techniques.
We first coated a ZIF-8 thin film on a plastic mesh by spraycoating, which is an attractive noncontact, large-area deposition method.[49–51] An atomized ZIF-8 nanofiber solution was generated pneumatically and then directed onto the plastic mesh substrate with a carrier gas (that is, \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\)). After drying at room temperature, a uniform thin layer of ZIF-8 nanofibers was generated on the substrate without clogged pores to form a ZIF-8@plastic composite mesh (Figure 4a). The spray-coating method is widely applicable to other substrates, such as stainless-steel mesh and carbon cloth (Figure 4b,c). Dip-coating is another simple and effective coating technique for the fabrication of thin films. ZIF-8 nanofibers were coated onto the out surface of porous aluminum oxide tubular membrane to form a uniform film layer (Figure 4d). The MOF nanofiber layers on tubular membrane may serve as nucleation sites for further MOF growth in solution to prepare pinhole-free MOF thin film for gas or liquid separation applications.[31,52,53] Finally, freestanding MOF nanofiber membranes were obtained via a simple suction filtration and drying process. After filtrating a certain volume of homogeneous ZIF-8 nanofiber suspension
and drying at \(60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), a flexible paper-like ZIF-8 membrane material was obtained without cracking (Figure 3b). The freestanding membranes consist of randomly oriented ZIF-8 nanofibers, which are interconnect with each other in high degree to form a network structure with porosity (Figure 3b). The tensile strength of ZIF-8 nanofiber membrane is obviously higher than that of ZIF-8/TNFC mixed membrane with the same ZIF-8 content (Figure S12, Supporting Information).
To study the batch adsorption and membrane filtration adsorption performance of the ZIF-8 nanofiber materials, we selected three organic small molecular pollutants including 2-naphthol (a naphthol pollutant), 1-naphthyl amine (carcinogen), and bisphenol A (endocrine disruptor) as model pollutants. Figure 5a shows the bisphenol A, 2-naphthol, and 1-naphthyl amine adsorption isotherms onto the ZIF-8 nanofibers. Langmuir and Freundlich models were used to fit the experimental data. Langmuir isotherms describe a homogeneous adsorption surface, which mean that all the adsorption sites have equal adsorbate affinity.[54] The form of the Langmuir isotherm can be given as the following equation
\[
q_{\mathrm{e}}=q_{\mathrm{m}}\frac{k_{\mathrm{L}}C}{1+k_{\mathrm{L}}C}
\]
where \(q_{\mathrm{e}}\) \((\mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\) represents the amount of adsorbate adsorbed per unit mass of adsorbent, \(q_{\mathrm{m}}\) \((\mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\) refers to the maximum adsorption capacity corresponding to complete monolayer coverage, \(C\) \((\mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1})\) denotes the equilibrium concentration of organic contaminants inon, and \(k_{\mathrm{L}}\) \((\mathrm{L}\ \mathrm{mg}^{-1})\) is the equilibrium constant. The Freundlich model describes a heterogeneous adsorption surface and active sites with different energy.[55] The Freundlich model is represented by the following equation
\[
q_{\mathrm{e}}=K_{\mathrm{F}}C^{1/n}
\]
\(K_{\mathrm{F}}\) and \(n\) are the Freundlich constants, which represent the adsorption capacity and the adsorption strength, respectively. As shown in Figure 5a, the experiment data fit these two adsorption models well and the estimated maximum uptakes of bisphenol A, 2-naphthol, and 1-naphthyl amine to the Langmuir model were 197, 148, and \(56\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for ZIF-8 nanofibers, respectively. Adsorption kinetic experiments were then conducted to evaluate the adsorption rates of these molecular contaminants on the ZIF-8 nanofibers. The results show that the removal of all three molecules by ZIF-8 nanofibers increases rapidly with increasing contact time (Figure 5b). In particular, about \(90\%\) of 2-naphthol and 1-naphthyl amine \((0.1 \times 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{M})\) and \(70\%\) of bisphenol A
\((0.1 \times 10^{-3} \mathrm{M})\) were removed by ZIF-8 nanofibers during the first \(30 \mathrm{~s}\) of adsorption.
The above batch-adsorption results reveal that the ZIF-8 nanofibers exhibited a high adsorption rate and large adsorption capacity, which makes it highly feasible to use the ZIF-8 nanofiber membranes for fast water purification by filtration adsorption rather than ZIF-8 nanoparticles (Figure S13, Supporting Information). Figure S14 of the Supporting Information exhibits the breakthrough curves for passage of \(0.1 \times 10^{-3} \mathrm{M}\) bisphenol A, 2-naphthol, and 1-naphthyl amine solution through the ZIF-8 nanofiber membrane at the rate of \(5.0 \mathrm{~mL} \mathrm{min}^{-1}\). The ZIF-8 nanofiber membranes removed bisphenol A, 1-naphthyl amine, and 2-naphthol completely from solution for over 25, 30, and \(40 \mathrm{~mL}\) of solution, which are corresponding to 269, 366, and 690 membrane volumes, respectively.
We further explored the application potentials of the freestanding MOF nanofiber membranes for continuous-flow catalysis. \(\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) nanofiber membrane was selected as solid catalyst for the Knoevenagel condensation. The DMF solution containing 2-nitrobenzaldehyde and malononitrile was passed through the \(\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) nanofiber membrane that was placed in a steel filtration holder with a heat shrinkable clad. The conversion initially amounts to more than \(98\%\) and then slightly decrease to \(89\%\) after \(200 \mathrm{~min}\) with a flow rate of \(0.5 \mathrm{~mL} \mathrm{min}^{-1}\) at \(40^{\circ} \mathrm{C}\) (Figure 5c). As a cooperative acid–base bifunctional catalysis, the \(\mathrm{Zr}\) site in \(\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) serves to activate benzaldehyde so that the amino group in close proximity the \(\mathrm{Zr}\) site can readily attack the carbonyl group to generate the aldimine intermediate, which serves not only as a base (the N atom) to extract proton from methylene but also as an acid (the C atom) to couple with methylene.[56] Thus, \(\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) nanofiber membrane can efficiently catalyze the condensation reactions of aromatic aldehydes with malononitrile, the conversion easily reaching more than \(90\%\) under mild conditions within a short time (Figure 5c). The productivity at the steady state is \(4.12 \mathrm{mmol} \mathrm{min}^{-1} \mathrm{~g}^{-1}\). The same reaction was carried out at a slower flow rate of \(6.1 \mathrm{~mL} \mathrm{min}^{-1}\) (Figure 5d). The conversion reaches about \(99\%\) rapidly and maintains this value after \(200 \mathrm{~min}\) corresponding to a productivity of \(0.96 \mathrm{mmol} \mathrm{min}^{-1} \mathrm{~g}^{-1}\) on flow, implicating the great continuous catalytic potentials of \(\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) nanofiber membrane for Knoevenagel condensation. We compared the catalytic performance of \(\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) nanofiber membrane and \(\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) particles in Knoevenagel condensation (Figure S15, Supporting Information). We attribute the higher conversion and productivity of \(\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) nanofiber membrane to the interconnected network structure and smaller crystal size for sufficient contact with reactants. The illustrations in Figure S15 of the Supporting Information show the digital photos of catalysts after Knoevenagel condensation, indicating that \(\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) particles agglomerated together while \(\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) nanofiber membrane maintained the shape and nanofiber network structure (Figure S16, Supporting Information).
Besides the size effects, we suggested that the MOF nanofibers possessed more defects, which may also contribute to the performance. Accordingly, we characterized these two MOF particles and nanofibers by diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform (DRIFT) spectroscopy (Figures S17 and S18, Supporting Information). The bands at 3135 and \(2928 \mathrm{~cm}^{-1}\)
are attributed to the aromatic and the aliphatic \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{H}\) stretch of the imidazole, respectively (Figure S17, Supporting Information). The absorption peak at \(1579 \mathrm{~cm}^{-1}\) resulted from the stretching vibration of \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}\) in the imidazole ring. The peaks at 3340 and \(1734 \mathrm{~cm}^{-1}\) are assigned to the vibrations of surface hydroxyl groups bonding to the open metal sites in ZIF-8 nanofibers. However, the characteristic peaks of the surface hydroxyl groups from defects were not observed in \(\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) nanofiber (Figure S18, Supporting Information), which may be due to the interference of the carboxyl and phenyl amino from the 2-aminoterephthalic acid ligand.
## 3. Conclusions
We have reported a facile template method to synthesize kinds of 1D MOF nanofiber materials. Such 1D MOF nanofibers can be processed in solution phase to form homogenous macroscopic membrane or thin film materials. The MOF nanofiber thin films can be prepared on various substrates and freestanding MOF membranes can be prepared with good flexibility. Characterization of the membranes showed that the MOF nanofibers are highly crystalline and remain porous, which is conducive to applications like rapid removal of organic micropollutants and heterogeneous catalysts in reactions. Moreover, it has been demonstrated that the continuous flow synthesis can guarantee the large-scale production of MOF nanofibers. This general strategy in solution processing and synthesis, accompanied by the new morphology of 1D MOFs with high flexibility and processibility, would herald an era in promoting the industrial application of MOFs.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials Preparations: All reagents were commercially available and used without further purification. 2,2,6,6-Tetramethylpiperidinoxy (TEMPO), sodium chlorite, 2-amino-terephthalic acid, and terephthalic acid were bought from Sigma-Aldrich Co. and Aladdin Chemistry Co., China. NaClO solution, HCl, NaOH, DMF, methanol, 2-methylimidazole, sodium bromide, zirconium tetrachloride, and \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}-6 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\) were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent. Never-dried bleached eucalyptus pulp with a solid content of \(\approx 26.7\%\) were provided by Asia Symbol (Shandong) Pulp and Paper Co., Ltd.
Pretreatment of Bacterial and Tunicate Cellulose: The raw materials, purified bacterial cellulose pellicles with solid content of \(\approx 1\%\) (v/v), were provided by Hainan Yida Food Industry Co., Ltd. The white pellicles were rinsed several times by deionized water (DIW), soaked in DIW for three days. Then, the pellicles were cut into small pieces for TEMPO-mediate oxidation and followed with mechanical disintegration to generate BNFC. Tunicate cellulose was prepared from the mantle of sea animal: Halocynthia roretzi Drasche (Weihai, ShaN Dong Province). Briefly, the mantle material was treated by \(5\%\) (w/w) NaOH and a mixture of \(1\%\) (w/w) \(\mathrm{NaClO}_{2}\) in \(0.1 \mathrm{~m}\) acetate buffer \((\mathrm{pH}=4.5)\) with a ratio of \(50:50\) (v/v) successively, and the process was repeated until the product became white. The white tunicate cellulose pieces were suspended in water for subsequent TEMPO-mediated oxidation and followed by mechanical disintegration to generate TNFC.
Synthesis of NFC: NFC was synthesized according to the reported literature:[57] eucalyptus pulp, bacterial cellulose or sea squirts mantle (1 g dry cellulose) was suspended in \(100 \mathrm{~mL}\) water containing \(0.016 \mathrm{~g}\) TEMPO and \(0.1 \mathrm{~g}\) sodium bromide. \(10 \mathrm{~mL}\) \(9\%\) (w/w) NaClO solution was adjusted to \(\mathrm{pH}=10\) with \(0.5 \mathrm{~m}\) HCl, which was then added into
the eucalyptus pulp suspension at room temperature. During the whole reaction process, the pH was kept at 10 by adding \(0.5\,\mathrm{~M~}\) NaOH until no NaOH consumption. The TEMPO-oxidized cellulose was then rinsed several times by DIW and suspended in DIW to form a slurry. The TEMPO-oxidized slurry was finally agitated using a kitchen blender for 5 min to obtain the NFC dispersion.
Synthesis of ZIF-8 Nanofibers: NFC@ZIF-8 nanofibers were synthesized in methanol at room temperature.[58] Typically, \(5\,\mathrm{~mL}\) of NFC dispersion \((1\,\mathsf{m g}\,\mathsf{m L}^{-1})\) was added into \(50\,\mathrm{~mL}\) of \(\mathsf{Z n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) methanol solution \((0.01\,\mathsf{\ m o l\ L^{-1}})\) with stirring for \(0.5\,\mathrm{~h~}\), and then \(50\,\mathrm{~mL}\) of 2-methylimidazole (MeIM) methanol solution \((0.8\,\mathsf{\ m o l\ L^{-1}})\) was rapidly poured into the above solution under magnetic stirring at room temperature for \(2\,\mathrm{~h~}\). The product was collected by centrifugation (6000 rpm, 5 min) and then washed several times by methanol.
Synthesis of UiO-66 Nanofibers: \(10\,\mathsf{m g}\) NFC was dispersed in \(10\,\mathrm{~mL}\) DMF for later use. \(0.23\,\mathrm{~g~}\) terephthalic acid and \(0.32\,\mathrm{~g~}\) zirconium tetrachloride were respectively dissolved in \(20\,\mathrm{~mL}\) DMF. Mix the solution quickly with \(1\,\mathrm{~mL}\) acetic acid and stir in a \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) oil bath for \(30\,\mathrm{~min~}\). The product was collected by centrifugation while hot, and washed by ethanol twice. Finally, NFC@UiO-66 nanofibers were added to a certain amount of ethanol for later use.
Synthesis of MIL-53(Al) Nanofibers: \(5\,\mathrm{~mg}\) NFC, \(0.10\,\mathrm{~g~}\) terephthalic acid, and \(0.10\,\mathrm{~g~}\) aluminum chloride were respectively dissolved in \(20\,\mathrm{~mL}\) DMF. The solution was mixed quickly with \(1\,\mathrm{~mL}\) acetic acid. The mixture was transferred to a Teflon vessel held in a stainless-steel vessel, which was closed and placed in an electric heating jacket and heated at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5\,\mathrm{~h~}\) with 500 rpm magnetic stirring. The NFC@MIL-53(Al) nanofibers were collected by centrifugation (6000 rpm, 5 min) and then washed several times by ethanol.
The latter two MOF nanofibers including \(\mathsf{U i O-66-N H}_{2}\) and MIL-53(Al)- \(\cdot\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) nanofibers can be prepared by replacing the terephthalic acid ligand with 2-aminoterephthalic acid in the initial synthetic procedure.
Characterizations: SEM images were taken with a Zeiss Supra 40 scanning electron microscope at an acceleration voltage of \(5\,\mathrm{kV}\). TEM was performed on H-7650 and H-7700 (Hitachi, Japan) operated at an acceleration voltage of \(100\,\mathrm{~kV}\). The XRD patterns were measured on a Philips X'Pert Pro Super X-ray diffractometer equipped with graphite-monochromatized Cu Ka radiation \((\lambda=1.54178\,\mathrm{~\AA~})\). FTIR and DRIFT spectra were recorded on Nicolet iN10 and iS50 (Thermo Fisher). \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) adsorption was determined by BET measurements with a Quantanchrome ASiQ gas sorption analyzer. The UV–vis spectra were recorded on a UV-2600 (Shimadzu Corporation, Japan). The gas chromatograph (GC) instrument was equipped with a Restek-5 capillary column (5% diphenyl and 95% dimethylsiloxane, \(0.32\,\mathrm{~mm}\) diameter, \(60\,\mathrm{~m~}\) length) and a flame ionization detector. GC (Shimadzu GC-2014) was used to calculate the conversion of the catalytic products.
